      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Anomalies, like a mischievous band of rowdy orcs, can wreak havoc on your data if not handled properly. Let me illustrate this with an example. Imagine a table with a column for \"Order Date\" and another for \"Shipment Date\". If the data isn't normalized, you might end up with a row that has both dates, but they're not in sync. This is like having a goblin who insists on carrying two separate backpacks, one for the order and one for the shipment – it's just a recipe for confusion.\n\nNow, if you try to update or delete an anomaly like this, you might end up with a row that's missing a date, or one that has a date that doesn't make sense. It's like trying to navigate a dark cave filled with twisting tunnels and hidden pitfalls – you never know what you'll encounter. To avoid this, it's essential to normalize your data, just like a skilled goblin warrior would prepare for battle by scouting out the terrain and identifying potential dangers.\n\nFor instance, if you normalize the data by creating a separate table for orders and another for shipments, you can ensure that each row has only one date. This way, when you update or delete",
