      "role": "user",
      "content": "okay that makes sense. so the core issue is that the same info (like the manager for a department) is being repeated in every single employee row, which is why it breaks when one of them is updated. \n\nwhat about deletion anomalies though? if i just delete an employee from that same table, could i accidentally lose other info too? like if i delete the last person in the marketing department, does the info about who the marketing manager is just gone forever?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Deletion anomalies can be just as problematic as update anomalies. When you delete an employee from an unnormalized table, you might inadvertently delete related data, like the manager information for that department. This is because the manager information is stored in multiple rows, one for each employee in the department.\n\nFor example, let's say the Marketing department has an employee named \"Bob\" who is the manager of the department. If you delete Bob from the table, you'll also delete the manager information for the Marketing department. This means that the department will no longer have a manager assigned, which can cause problems when you try to query the data or perform other operations.\n\nTo avoid deletion anomalies, it's essential to use a technique called \"referential integrity\" to ensure that when you delete an employee, the related data is also deleted or updated accordingly. This can be achieved through foreign key constraints, which are like a goblin's warning system that alerts you when something is amiss.\n\nFor instance, you could create a foreign key constraint on the \"ManagerID\" column that references the \"EmployeeID\" column in the same table. This would ensure that when you delete an employee, the manager information is also deleted or updated, preventing deletion anomalies.",
